# Print surface lock (Cindy, Aug 30 2026)

## Why this exists

The print/PDF layer in `cch-proposals-invoices-fix.js`, `index.html` and `client.html` is
shared by **four document types: proposals, invoices, purchase orders, tear sheets.**

A change aimed at one of them silently reaches the other three.

Confirmed cases:

- **9.9.278** (Aug 27, straight to production): "Print/PDF: drop 850px page lock, page 2
  top + left inset." Its verify line read *"Verify: PRO-3044 Print"*. It was checked against
  a proposal. PO Print inherited the inset and printed a dead band. Found three days later
  on PO-9045 with a vendor waiting.
- **9.9.214** (Aug 17): Ship To restyled to a bold name plus a 12px gray caption "to match
  vendor." Nobody asked for it. It made the job address on every PO read as a footnote.
- **9.9.275 / 9.9.276** (Aug 27): Room/Tag columns and the Show room / Show tags toggles
  were added, gated to `type === 'proposal' || type === 'invoice'`. POs were excluded, so on
  a PO those fields still stack under ITEM. This reads to Cindy as the fix "reverting"
  when it was never applied to that surface.

## Rules

1. **Staging only.** A print-layer change does not take a same-session production GO.
   It goes to staging, gets the four-document verify, and ships to production in a later
   pass. Print defects surface days later in front of a client or a vendor, which is
   exactly when the "I need it now" instinct is most expensive.

2. **Verify the set, not the trigger.** Every print-layer change is verified against
   **all four**: one PO, one sent invoice, one sent proposal, one tear sheet. Naming a
   single document in the verify line is not sufficient and is how 9.9.278 shipped.

3. **State the surfaces in the WO.** The work order says which of the four the change is
   intended to affect and which it must not. If you cannot answer that, you have not
   grounded the change yet.

4. **No uncommissioned restyling.** Do not change type size, weight, color, stacking or
   spacing on Bill To, Ship To, or the line-item block to make one document "match"
   another. Layout parity is a design decision and it is Cindy's, not an implementation detail.

5. **Ship To renders verbatim.** The Ship To block prints the field exactly as typed in
   Edit PO, preserving line breaks. Nothing downstream re-parses it, reflows it to one
   line, or inserts separator characters.

## Before editing any `@media print`, `@page`, Bill To / Ship To, or line-item block

Grep the gate you are about to change and list every `type ===` branch it sits inside.
If a gate names some document types and not others, say so in chat before editing, because
that asymmetry is usually the actual bug rather than the thing you were sent to fix.
